WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:03.120
We live in a world saturated with misinformation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:07.120
so it's no surprise that we get things&nbsp;
wrong from time to time. Sit back and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:11.600
find out all the false things you've come to&nbsp;
believe about why these shows were cancelled.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:17.120
Years after Felicity left the airwaves,&nbsp;
it's still remembered for one event:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:21.920
The Haircut. More than any character,&nbsp;
plot point, or storyline, Felicity's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:26.160
iconic hair getting trimmed into a&nbsp;
weird sort of pixie cut has defined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.160
the series. It's been a source of&nbsp;
pop culture derision ever since,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:35.120
and it's often considered the moment that killed&nbsp;
the show. There is a little truth to this, being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.720
that there was enough fan backlash that executive&nbsp;
producer J.J. Abrams took responsibility for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:46.480
idea in the press. However, the real problems&nbsp;
were changing time slots and weaker stories.

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:52.000
First, the time slot. Felicity was broadcast&nbsp;
on Tuesday evenings during the first season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:58.240
prime TV real estate for the late '90s. The second&nbsp;
season saw the network move it to Sunday nights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:02.320
a much weaker time slot. Second,&nbsp;
the storylines were underwhelming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:05.840
an opinion held by both fans&nbsp;
and creatives working on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:10.320
show. Abrams admitted this at the time&nbsp;
to the Pittsburgh Post-Gazette, saying:

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.400
"There was a crucial period of four or&nbsp;
five episodes where we told stories that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.400
were a little unfocused and it didn't&nbsp;
feel as compelling as it should have."

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.080
His estimate of four or five is&nbsp;
important, because the Haircut&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.440
happened in episode five. It might have&nbsp;
shocked some fans, but the show was already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:32.800
losing viewers — and it's easier to blame a&nbsp;
single event than a series of bad decisions.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:39.360
The final season of Zoey 101 was shown under&nbsp;
the spectre of one of Nickelodeon's biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:45.760
controversies: star Jamie Lynn Spears becoming&nbsp;
pregnant at age 16. Given that she announced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:52.160
her pregnancy in December of 2007 and the&nbsp;
fourth and final season began January 2008,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:55.600
many see the two events and&nbsp;
assume one led to the finale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.720
of the other. This is false — the two events&nbsp;
were, in fact, independent of one another.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:05.600
Series creator Dan Schneider gets asked if&nbsp;
Spears' pregnancy led to the end of the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.320
quite a bit, so much so that he put&nbsp;
the answer on his blog's FAQ page.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:14.880
He made it abundantly clear that they didn't&nbsp;
even find out she was pregnant until later.

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:20.240
"The last season was shot during the summer of&nbsp;
2007, and wrapped in August. We had our final&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:24.800
wrap party at the end of August. Everyone&nbsp;
had a fun time, hugged, and we all said our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:29.760
goodbyes. That was the end of Zoey 101, in&nbsp;
terms of production. It wasn't until several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:34.640
weeks later (in October) that anyone from our&nbsp;
production heard about Jamie Lynn being pregnant."

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.840
He also said the show ended&nbsp;
because Nick had enough in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:41.040
can for reruns and everyone was ready to move on.

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:47.280
One of the central narratives NBC pushed in&nbsp;
the wake of the 2010 Tonight Show conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.640
was that Conan O'Brien's run as the host of&nbsp;
the show led to the long-running franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.000
losing money for the first time in its history.&nbsp;
Somehow, despite all the public examination&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:01.600
and re-litigation, this point has&nbsp;
stood. Here's the thing, though:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.600 --> 00:03:04.880
it's mathematically improbable&nbsp;
for the show to have lost money.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:15.840
For starters, television isn't about having the&nbsp;
most viewers, it's about having the right viewers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:21.600
While O'Brien's Tonight was drawing fewer overall&nbsp;
viewers, he outperformed Leno's tenure in key&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:27.280
demographics — a surefire sign of better ad&nbsp;
buys and high revenue. Speaking of revenue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:32.240
lower revenue doesn't mean unprofitable:&nbsp;
O'Brien's Tonight had similar ratings to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:36.960
The Late Show with David Letterman, a show that&nbsp;
cost more and was still profitable. O'Brien's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:43.040
salary was also estimated to be just half&nbsp;
of Leno's $30 million. On top of all this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:47.520
talk shows are so durable because they're&nbsp;
among the cheapest television shows to produce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.520 --> 00:03:52.000
and the idea that the biggest name show in&nbsp;
late night would lose money is unlikely.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:57.360
O'Brien told 60 Minutes that NBC's claim&nbsp;
about Tonight losing money was impossible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:03.040
a sentiment echoed by late night experts and&nbsp;
experts consulted by The Wrap. O'Brien reasoned in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:08.800
Bill Carter's book The War For Late Night that NBC&nbsp;
could have only decided the show was unprofitable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:14.080
if they factored in construction of new offices&nbsp;
and sets, which is a stretch to say the least.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:19.280
Strangers with Candy frequently appears&nbsp;
on lists of shows canceled too soon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:23.040
Running for 30 episodes between 1999 and 2000,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:28.160
the show had a murderers' row of comedic&nbsp;
talent — Amy Sedaris, Stephen Colbert,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:34.720
Paul Dinello, and Janeane Garofalo— and a strong&nbsp;
cult following. Fans rejoiced when a prequel film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:40.720
came out in 2005 starring all the original cast.&nbsp;
During the press tour, Sedaris revealed something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:45.680
that didn't get much attention then, nor has&nbsp;
it since: the show was never canceled at all.

00:04:45.680 --> 00:04:47.840
In an interview with the Austin Chronicle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:51.360
Sedaris stated that at no point&nbsp;
was the show ever canceled:

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:56.000
"We wanted to go out; we wanted a final&nbsp;
episode because usually Comedy Central does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:00.080
things in three seasons, so we kind of&nbsp;
figured we had three seasons. We thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:05.120
if we were going to do it again, yeah, we could&nbsp;
write 10 episodes or not write 10 episodes."

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:09.840
They just stopped making episodes,&nbsp;
figuring that their time was up anyway.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.840
The Wonder Years came to a natural conclusion,&nbsp;
both in the show's continuity and with real life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:21.040
circumstances. In the show, Kevin and Winnie's&nbsp;
relationship comes to a passionate close and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:26.080
everyone is ready to move on to their next&nbsp;
chapter. In real life, all of the young actors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.080 --> 00:05:30.480
were getting older and the show had to wrap up&nbsp;
sooner rather than later. But according to at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:35.760
least one source, these aren't the true reasons&nbsp;
the show ended: the blame lies with a lawsuit.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:39.520
"My hunch was that my father would&nbsp;
not let being called a brain-washed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:42.320
moron at his dinner table go by unremarked."

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:48.240
During the final season, the show's former&nbsp;
costume designer Monique Long filed a sexual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.200
harassment suit against stars Fred Savage and&nbsp;
Jason Hervey, claiming she was let go from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:59.120
show after rebuffing their advances. Abbey Mills,&nbsp;
who played Norma Arnold, revealed in a 2018&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:03.120
interview with Yahoo! Entertainment that&nbsp;
the PR nightmare that would have resulted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:08.320
led the show to make the final episode of that&nbsp;
season the series finale instead of hanging on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.320 --> 00:06:13.520
for another year. Mills refuted the suit, which&nbsp;
was later settled out of court. Savage has long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:18.880
defended his innocence, but Long stands by her&nbsp;
claims. Either way, there's credible reason to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:24.160
believe this legal action had more to do with&nbsp;
the show getting axed than Savage turning 17.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:31.120
The arc of Twin Peaks as a show, from debut&nbsp;
to cancellation, is often summed up thusly:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.120 --> 00:06:35.120
the first season was a smash hit, the&nbsp;
network forced the show to reveal the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.840
killer early after dwindling interest, and&nbsp;
the rest of season two just wasn't that good.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.880
Every part of this is at least partially true, and&nbsp;
there were other factors that fed into it — such&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:49.520
as a behind-the-scenes feud that killed the&nbsp;
planned Audrey-Cooper storyline. But this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:55.040
all leaves out maybe the most important reason&nbsp;
for the ratings drop: time slot shenanigans.

00:06:55.040 --> 00:07:00.720
The first episode of Twin Peaks, broadcast on&nbsp;
a Sunday, nabbed monster ratings — partially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:06.160
due to America's Funniest Home Videos being the&nbsp;
lead-in. Ratings stayed solid enough after it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:11.680
moved to Thursdays, but there was a clear decline&nbsp;
in viewership after four episodes. This was due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.680 --> 00:07:18.000
in part to ABC scheduling Twin Peaks against&nbsp;
Cheers, a well-established powerhouse of a show.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.000 --> 00:07:23.920
Cheers was also episodic instead of serialized&nbsp;
like Twin Peaks, meaning that someone who missed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:29.280
an episode of Twin Peaks might be more willing&nbsp;
to cut their losses in an era before DVR.

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:32.080
The situation was made worse in season two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:37.280
being that it was moved to Saturday nights,&nbsp;
one of the worst time slots on all of TV.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:42.960
Series co-creator Mark Frost brought up&nbsp;
another obstacle during an interview on WFDU,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:48.640
which was that the show kept getting preempted by&nbsp;
coverage of the Gulf War, a huge moneymaker and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:54.800
ratings getter for TV at the time. Between the bad&nbsp;
time slot, the preemption, and the serialization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.800 --> 00:08:00.240
in an era when that wasn't common, the show was&nbsp;
going to lose viewers regardless of its content.

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:05.840
The 1980s Dungeons &amp; Dragons cartoon&nbsp;
came out at the initial height of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:11.360
game's popularity — and was a big hit for a&nbsp;
few seasons. It also attracted controversy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:17.760
almost entirely because D&amp;D was one of the targets&nbsp;
of the 1980s Satanic panic. The most prominent of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.760 --> 00:08:22.960
these objections came from the National Coalition&nbsp;
of Television Violence, who petitioned the Federal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:28.560
Trade Commission and Consumer Protection Agency&nbsp;
to put a warning on all D&amp;D properties — including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.560 --> 00:08:33.040
the TV show — that they were linked to&nbsp;
violent deaths. These requests were rejected.

00:08:33.040 --> 00:08:38.000
Between the D&amp;D panic and the concerns about&nbsp;
violence, the fact that the show ended with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:43.760
unresolved storyline after three seasons fed into&nbsp;
two rumors about its cancellation, both of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:49.120
false. First, that the missing episode showed&nbsp;
the kids suffering an awful fate — often listed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:55.200
as being stuck in Hell after dying. Script writer&nbsp;
Michael Reaves shot this down hard on his blog.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:58.880
Second, that the show was canceled&nbsp;
after protests. Mark Evanier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.880 --> 00:09:02.960
who developed the show for television,&nbsp;
dismissed that on his blog, explaining:

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:08.320
"The Dungeons &amp; Dragons series was cancelled due&nbsp;
to declining ratings. I keep reading stories about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.320 --> 00:09:12.640
it getting yanked off the air because it was&nbsp;
too 'violent' or because protest groups thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:18.080
it was Satanic or something of the sort. As far&nbsp;
as I know, the protests were minimal and had no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:23.120
impact. The show simply began losing its&nbsp;
audience, as all shows eventually do."

00:09:23.120 --> 00:09:27.760
"Good, but make sure you wait until I fire the&nbsp;
signal arrow before you begin your attack."

00:09:28.876 --> 00:09:35.200
The mere phrase "Cousin Oliver" inspires snarled&nbsp;
lips and looks of disdain among sitcom lovers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:39.680
Brought in during the last season of The Brady&nbsp;
Bunch, Oliver was meant to replace some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:45.680
cute factor that was lost after the youngest kids&nbsp;
grew up — but instead ended up a cloying presence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:51.120
None of this is a knock on actor Robbie Rist, who&nbsp;
was just a kid and went on to have a solid career&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:56.640
in music and voice acting. Nonetheless, "Cousin&nbsp;
Oliver" is now shorthand for when shows bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:02.320
in someone cute to liven up a dying show and the&nbsp;
new character ends up having the inverse effect.

00:10:02.320 --> 00:10:04.240
There's just one problem with that theory:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.240 --> 00:10:09.120
The Brady Bunch was not popular during its&nbsp;
original run, not by any meaningful metric.

00:10:09.120 --> 00:10:13.840
Though the show surely had its regular viewers,&nbsp;
at no point during its five-season run did it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.840 --> 00:10:20.720
crack the Top 30 of Nielsen ratings, peaking at 31&nbsp;
in season three. The final season was rated 55th&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:26.560
out of a possible 80 shows, and at that point&nbsp;
no cast addition or subtraction is going to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:31.680
a difference — and it's unfair to blame a kid,&nbsp;
albeit an obnoxious one, who was only there for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.680 --> 00:10:37.360
handful of episodes. There's also a decent chance&nbsp;
ABC wouldn't have wanted to continue anyway:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.360 --> 00:10:42.240
not only were the kids getting older, they&nbsp;
had enough episodes for syndication. If so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:47.040
that plan worked beautifully: it became&nbsp;
a monster hit and TV staple in reruns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:50.080
never leaving the air and&nbsp;
kickstarting a new franchise.

00:10:50.960 --> 00:10:57.040
The phrase "jump the shark" has a special meaning&nbsp;
to Happy Days fans. Named after an absurd stunt in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.040 --> 00:11:02.720
season five episode "Hollywood: Part 3" where&nbsp;
Fonzie straps on water skis and jumps over a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:08.320
shark, the idiom refers to the point where a show&nbsp;
does something that marks a decline in quality.

00:11:08.320 --> 00:11:13.680
For Happy Days in particular, the shark jump&nbsp;
wasn't even close to the beginning of the end.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:18.160
It wasn't even the beginning of the&nbsp;
middle. It ran for years afterwards.

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:23.840
Happy Days was never a critical darling, so the&nbsp;
idea that reviews went down afterwards is hard to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.840 --> 00:11:30.560
substantiate. Also, the phrase "jump the shark"&nbsp;
was coined circa 1985 — at least one year after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.560 --> 00:11:36.320
Happy Days ended and nearly a decade after the&nbsp;
episode in question. Having such a moment marked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.320 --> 00:11:41.280
in hindsight instead of contemporarily shows&nbsp;
this as more of a thought experiment than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:46.240
hard and fast rule. The franchise also&nbsp;
had a number of important events occur&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:51.680
after the jump, including the introduction&nbsp;
of Mork from Ork and a half dozen spinoffs.

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:56.320
Fred Fox Jr., who wrote the episode,&nbsp;
made an airtight point in the L.A. Times:

00:11:56.320 --> 00:12:01.920
"It was the 91st episode and the fifth season.&nbsp;
If this was really the beginning of a downward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:08.800
spiral, why did the show stay on the air for six&nbsp;
more seasons and shoot an additional 164 episodes?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:13.440
Why did we rank among the Top 25&nbsp;
in five of those six seasons?"

00:12:13.440 --> 00:12:17.440
MeTV even offered their own list&nbsp;
of more accurate alternatives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:20.640
including Fonzie working as&nbsp;
a teacher and kissing a nun.

00:12:21.760 --> 00:12:29.040
Television in the 1950s and 60s was defined by&nbsp;
shows taking place in rural areas and small towns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:29.040 --> 00:12:33.920
Green Acres, Petticoat Junction, The Beverly&nbsp;
Hillbillies, and more than a few shows about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.920 --> 00:12:38.640
the town of Mayberry ruled the airwaves&nbsp;
— and that's just scratching the surface.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:44.560
Then there was a sea change around 1970, when&nbsp;
networks saw urban shows like All in the Family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.560 --> 00:12:49.360
and Mary Tyler Moore draw great ratings&nbsp;
and bring a new level of respectability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.360 --> 00:12:55.920
and sophistication to the networks. By the&nbsp;
end of the 1970 and 1971 TV season, most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.920 --> 00:13:01.680
these country-themed shows were unceremoniously&nbsp;
canceled in what's been dubbed the Rural Purge.

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:06.160
One such show that wrapped during this&nbsp;
era was Gomer Pyle, U.S.M.C. – and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.160 --> 00:13:10.880
given its connection to Mayberry, is often&nbsp;
incorrectly listed as getting canceled as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.880 --> 00:13:15.520
part of the Rural Purge. This is false,&nbsp;
being that star Jim Nabors left the show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:21.840
in 1969 after five wildly successful seasons&nbsp;
simply because he wanted to do something else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:27.280
Nabors saw himself as more of an entertainer than&nbsp;
an actor, and as such he built a variety show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.280 --> 00:13:31.280
after departing. There was, however,&nbsp;
one related show that got axed:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.280 --> 00:13:36.640
Mayberry R.F.D., the last connection to The&nbsp;
Andy Griffith Show remaining. It's likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.640 --> 00:13:42.880
Mayberry R.F.D. and Gomer Pyle, U.S.M.C.&nbsp;
got conflated and a misconception was born.

00:13:42.880 --> 00:13:45.680
